Date: Wed, 07 Jul 93 07:53:45 GMT
From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Subject: NANO: Drexler style nanotech is unworkable

James Donald
>> Drexler's his conception of Van der Waals forces is false. 
>> Van der Waals forces directly cause static friction,
>> independent of imperfections.  Making and breaking contact
>> between atoms involves substantial grabbiness and non
>> uniformity.
Eli Brandt
> "VdW forces directly cause static friction" -- what do you mean by
> this?  Your previous use of terms such as "energy hole" suggests that
> you agree that these forces are conservative.  So where's the friction
> come in? If it ain't dissipative, it's not "friction" in my book.

Van der Waals forces are not conservative, although energy holes and
bumps do exist on surfaces.  The shape of the surface (the electron
distribution relative to the nuclei) and the interatomic separations
alter in the a VdW's interaction.  Hence linear energy of motion is
transferred to internal vibrational modes (ie friction is generated) as
surfaces slide over eachother.  And to go back to an earlier point James
made, VdW is a r^-6 force, so these effects are powerful down on the
nanoscale.

All-in-all I agree with the points James is making about Drexlerian
nanotech.  VdW's forces are the result of mutually induced atomic
distortions and deviations from symmetry.  As such it would not surprise
me to find out that these hard-to-calculate subtleties have been ignored
in the calculations.  This is suggested by the statements about surfaces
smoothly sliding over each other.

I am inclined to agree, without detailed knowledge, with the point James
Donald makes about the inappropriateness of scaling down macro designs
to the nano-scale.  I, too, find the classical concepts of axles, gears,
bearings, rods, etc to be highly unconvincing at the molecular scale. 
To borrow a metaphor I know James is keen on, this does strike me as a
case of doing calculations around the base of the street lamp (of
classical physics and design) when the keys (a viable molecular machine)
are lost in the outer darkness (of molecular quantum space).

I believe some of the paradigms in nanotech are inappropriate.  These
are:
1)   classical rod-and-stick designs
2)   the nanotech self-replicator assembler

More on point 2) later.

Mike Price          price@price.demon.co.uk           AS member

