> bearings, rods, etc to be highly unconvincing at the molecular scale. 
> To borrow a metaphor I know James is keen on, this does strike me as a
> case of doing calculations around the base of the street lamp (of
> classical physics and design) when the keys (a viable molecular machine)
> are lost in the outer darkness (of molecular quantum space).

Oh come on, Price! You've got to look where the lights are
_brightest_, not where the keys were lost!

Seriously, I have been reading James Donald's comments with great
interest, though my physical chemistry is so weak--it's been 25 years
since I last took chemistry!--I can't judge his arguments.

What I _do_ think is that this line of reasoning, about the nature of
rubbing, friction, van der Waals forces, etc., gets to the core of
"Nanosystems" (which I hope James D. can quickly get a hold of, the
better to analyze Drexler's actual arguments) and is potentially the
type of debate we sorely need.

(As I have said before, Eric Drexler frequently writes about
evolutionary epistemology, pancritical rationalism, and the
desirability of "decision duels," "fact forums," and "science courts."
And yet, for whatever reasons, there have been none of these in
connection with nanotech. The nanotech conferences have *not* seen
this kind of active debate, with critics like James Donald making
their case. To be fair to Eric and the organizers, I have no reason to
believe they turned away anyone because of his views or his plans to
criticize nanotech basics.)

So, perhaps the issues raised by James Donald, or the issues of
whether nanoscale machines will "freeze up" or not (this seems to be a
key point), could be the basis of an Extropians list "virtual
seminar," like the one we've been doing on algorithmic information
theory. That is, readings of papers and books, some calculations, etc.

Also, James Donald should get involved in sci.nanotech. A friend of
mine, Howard Landman, is quite active there and is a computational
chemist, so he may be very well prepared to analyze the points.

As we've never built even the simplest gear or bearing system out of
molecular components (carbyne rods, canonically), we can't test the
competing theories, but the predicted outcomes seem very testable:
Drexlers claims the machines will freely rotate, Donald claims they
will freeze up. (This is, to make another point completely, a good
example of Popperian falsifiability in action.)

Mike Price goes on:

> I believe some of the paradigms in nanotech are inappropriate.  These
> are:
> 1)   classical rod-and-stick designs
> 2)   the nanotech self-replicator assembler
> 
> More on point 2) later.

The difficulty--nay, the utterly unknown nature of--of #2, designing a
mechanical self-replicator, has interested me for several years, as
readers know.

It's hard enough doing it mechanically, in a deterministic system. If
we cannot build molecular machines (classical, gears and bearings,
etc.), and have to do it with bags of jellies and lipids and folding
of proteins....the Singularity just got rescheduled ("Don't call us,
we'll call you.")

AIT and logical depth enters here, as evolution has almost certainly
programmed in a lot of complicated, obscure, nonobvious tricks, which
we'll need to understand before duplicating them.

I look forward to hearing more on this topic.

-Tim May



