Date: Wed, 7 Jul 93 9:14:09 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: NANO: Drexler style nanotech is unworkable

James Donald:

> I have raised specific facts:  That there exist molecules
> analogous to the mechanisms proposed by Drexler, for
> example HOPG, and fullerene spheres sitting on HOPG planes,
> and they do not move around freely from thermal motion, as
> would follow from Drexler's assumptions, they freeze up, as
> Drexlers unlubricated nanomachines would freeze up.

My knowledge of physics isn't very large, which is why I haven't
taken up this matter in detail.  It is quite important, however --
so if you don't mind explaining a little bit in a dialog that
may take some time, we should be able to shed some more light.
Far as I can tell so far, the current acrimony is caused by
that most frustrating of intellectual blockages:  a conflict
of intuitions.  Pardon the elementary nature of the following
comments.

As I understand it, VdW force is an electrostatic attractive
force that falls off in strength by r^6.  Another important
force at this scale is "overlap repulsion" caused (I presume)
by electron clouds pushing against each other or some such
thing.

In _Nanosystems_, Drexler devotes a major portion of his analysis
(and subsequent design applications) to regimes where these
forces are dominant.

Your argument is that this analysis is incorrect, apparently
because drexler treats VdW as a "pairwise" force.  What
else could it be?  In 3.5.1.a, he talks about an assumption
of only considering "nonretarded" interactions.  Is this
where the mistake is made?  The problem is if you want us
to address issues you raise, the immediate response is to point
to Nanosystems, since it seems he took great care to do
impressive (that is, well-typeset) analysis; criticism can't
be on general issues, because it seems to many of us unwashed
that D. has addressed general issues.  Where is he wrong, that's
what we'd like to know.

You offer an example of where he _might_ be wrong, dealing
with the fact that (I think I remember right) in some uncited
experiment buckyballs didn't roll around on graphite surfaces
the way that they should, according to Drexler -- instead they
stuck.  The inference is that the parts of nanomachines would
get gummed up in a similar way.

That's an interesting _possibility_ for a criticism, but is
hardly at all convincing at present.  First, we need several
things.  We need a reference to the experiment in question,
so we can investigate the circumstances:  what exactly was
the surface like, what were the surrounding conditions, what
impurities, how many of this and that, etc.  Once we have that,
we should be able to apply Drexler's analysis in a straightforward
and numerical way to see what the real prediction of drexler's
analysis were.  Until that is done, it does no good to claim
that the situation is "analogous" to the mechanisms drexler
designs later in his book -- we don't know whether this case
is analogous or not.

We can go from there to determine the extent of the consequences.

derek


