Date: Wed, 7 Jul 93 2:02:07 EDT
From: boojum!esr@gvls1.VFL.Paramax.COM (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: PAGANISM: Portrait of the Artist as a Young Pagan

In a recent posting, Michael Clive Price asked what the benefits of Wicca are.

It's hard for me to write about the benefits of Wicca in the abstract.  My
experience with it has been an intensely personal one, bound up with my
life history in complicated and delicate ways.  I tried, but I found myself
drifting into personal history every time I attempted to supply a referent.

So, instead, here's a sketch of my life as a pagan, and what I learned there.

My first gift from the Gods was music.

I was eighteen, I guess.  I'd taken up guitar perhaps a year earlier, learned
to bash out a few standards.  I wasn't very good at it; my mind kept running
ahead of my clumsy hands.  Despite having an excellent ear and an almost
phonographic memory for music, I felt stuck at a low level.  Couldn't sing
while playing; couldn't compose; couldn't seem to get through all the
mechanical trivia of tablatures and fingerings and picking patterns to the
effortless communion of player and instrument I saw in really good musicians.

Then, one day, I picked up my little sister's flute.  And I began to play.
And, friends, I was *good*.  Instantly.  So good that my sister surrendered
the instrument without hardly a murmur.

About this time, I went off to college.  I took that flute with me everywhere;
played it walking between classes.  Within a couple months after taking it up,
I was jamming with professional jazz and rock bands in West Philly --- and
getting applause.  Two guitarists invited me to join their band and we
developed a bit of a following doing basement gigs in the Penn Quadrangle.

Something happened to me when I played that flute.  The angry, anxious,
frustrated adolescent kid I was then just disappeared.  I was powerful,
competent, passionate.  And I got these amazing rushes of pure timeless joy,
when my consciousness seemed to expand outwards from the limits of my skin to
fill the universe and I could no longer tell whether I was playing the
music or the music was playing me.

Nor were these effects just going on inside my own head.

At the University of Pennsylvania Museum of Anthropology there's a large,
circular domed chamber in the Oriental wing with a skylight at its apex.
Directly underneath that skylight they display the second largest
natural-crystal globe in the world, a flawless five-inch sphere on a silver
stand in the form of a dragon.  I found it there one day with the sun shining
full on it.  Feeling utterly compelled, I ran ten blocks and back to get my
flute, gazed into it, and started playing.

I don't know how long I played.  When I was aware of the world again, echoes
were dying away in the high dome, and a dozen or so people were lined up at
the four entrances, keeping *absolutely* still.  Rather stupidly, I said "Why
are you all standing there?" thinking that if they'd wanted to listen they
certainly could have entered the room.  And a man said "We didn't want to
interrupt you..." in a hushed, totally awed tone of voice that embarrassed
me no end.  We all looked at each other like we'd been caught in some weird
intimacy in public.  I mumbled something and fled.

And this kind of thing *kept happening*.  Without my intention.  Without my
understanding it.  Until the girl on the landing.

I was climbing the stair to the fourth floor in one of the quad houses one
day, to visit some friends.  A girl stepped onto the landing, saw me, and
turned white as a sheet.  I said something obvious along the lines of "Why are
you looking at me like that?" and she started to babble something about last
week when she was studying, looking outside at the quad and seeing me walk by
"...and the leaves were following you!"  And it was like I was the Spring and
the life in the grass.  And a whole bunch of other stuff that made me wonder
what drugs she'd been doing (this was 1976 or early '77; every second dorm
room had a bong and blotter acid was easier to score than good music).  So I
shook my head dubiously and rolled on upstairs and visited my buddies.

I was walking home when I got it.  That girl had looked at me and seen a god.

I was just the kind of dogmatic atheist you'd expect a bright, scientifically
inclined kid with a parochial-school education to have turned into.  I knew
the score; religion was an instrument of oppression, a drug for the stupid
and credulous.  Mysticism?  Garbage.

But I had an omnivorous curiousity, and one of my friends was "into the
occult".  Had a chin beard, read Crowley, wore black turtlenecks.  He loved to
talk endlessly about Eastern mysticism and archetypal psychology, shamanism,
the organizing role of ritual, magic as mind-change, metaprogramming (I sat
for him once while he did LSD).  A minister's son; turned into a
Fundamentalist with an alcohol problem years later.  But he was very
educational at the time.  I started to see that there was a kind of live
internal logic to mysticism and religion, something entwined with psychology
that sent runners and shoots all through art.

Not that I took any of it seriously as a description of the real world.  It
was an intellectual chew-toy, at best a way of understanding the pathologies
that prevented human beings from living the infinitely more desirable life
of reason and science.

Until I realized, finally, belatedly, what had been happening to me.  Until
the Great God Pan reached out of my hindbrain and thundered "YOU!"  And his
gift is music and his chosen instruments the pipes and flutes.  And his, too
the power of joy; magic so strong that when it flowed out of me, even before I
knew what I was doing, it amazed people into awe and incoherence and poetry.

That day I was reborn; from a skinny lame kid with a flute into a shaman and a
vessel of the Goat-Foot God, the Piper at the Gates of Dawn, the Horned Lord.
And the music was my first power.

(And, oh, yes.  The first time I handled a set of pan-pipes I could play them.
Fluently.  Effortlessly.  And knew I could before I touched them.)

Now.  If my language is too "religious" for you, feel free to transpose it all
into the key of psychology.  Feel free to hypothesize that I'd spontaneously
learned how to enter a non-ordinary mental state that changed my body language,
disabled a few mental censors, and had me putting out signals that other
people interpreted in terms of certain material in their own unconscious minds.

Fine.  You've explained it.  Correctly, even.  But you can't *do* it!

And as long as you stick with the sterile denotative language of psychology,
and the logical mode of the waking mind, you won't be able to --- because you
can't reach and program the unconscious that way.  It takes music, symbolism,
sex, hypnosis, wine and strange drugs, firelight and chanting, ritual and
magic.  Super-stimuli that reach past the conscious mind and neocortex, in and
back to the primate and mammal and reptile brains curled up inside.

My second gift from the Gods was sex.

I was a horny teenge kid who'd just discovered that the frippin' God of Sex
Himself had chosen to manifest through me, big-time.  What do you *think* I
did with all that mana? :-)

My sex life hadn't been much before then.  When I figured out how to evoke the
God voluntarily, that changed.  Fast.  Radically.  I got to be good at
attracting women.  And at pleasing them.  Around this time I discovered SF
fandom, and it was like being loose with a fistful of bucks in a candy store.
All I had to do was be Himself...

I had women by the score.  I had them by twos and threes.  At one point in
1983 I was living with two women and semi-regularly seeing about four others.
I hasten to add that there was no deception or concealment involved (and I'm
still living happily with one of those two women ten years later).

I've slowed down since then.  I'm 35 now and my hormone level isn't quite as
high as it used to be.  And the fear of AIDS has squashed a lot of the casual
polyvalent hedonism that used to be common in SF fandom and elsewhere.  But
Pan's second gift is still with me; I expect to be sexually vigorous and
interesting to women until the day I die.  All women have the nature of the
Goddess and it is the Horned One's destiny and delight to love them.

(And I do mean *love*, adolescents and male-chauvinist-porkers of the world.
I have loved in some measure every woman I've ever slept with, because I have
been open to love.  The God permits no less in any man who truly embodies him;
to use and despise women is to deny their Goddess-nature and to drive out the
God and his potential power in oneself.)

(An opposite and equal truth holds for women...)

My third gift from the Gods was poetry.

Around 1979 I found that I was beginning to be able to compose for voice and
guitar.  I'd always been able to sing, albeit with a narrow range (I like to
joke that I have exactly the same five-note range as Gordon Lightfoot.  The
second implied claim in the joke is true...).  My guitar playing got better
after I came into my power, not as good as my flute but once I understood what
was happening I also understood that it would never be.

I soon discovered that my best mode of composition was the pagan mythological
epic.  I wrote all the usual singer-songwriter stuff, ersatz folk music and
confessional ballads and she-done-me-wrong songs and Neil-Youngoid trippy
music (even though I never did drugs).  I had quite a rep at one time for
turning out clever examples of the kind of punny pastiche SF fandom calls a
"filk song".

All this, though, was ephemera.  I've forgotten half of it myself.  I've
attained true poetry --- the timeless, resonant imagery that transforms what
it touches into inevitable beauty --- only in my pagan songs.  They came
through me in a process almost like automatic writing --- composed at
white heat, with images jetting their way into my consciousness as though
under tremendous back-pressure, like water roaring through a narrow firehose
nozzle.  At times only the prosody was under my conscious control.

The very process of composing them was exhausting, and the results are so
intense that they are barely within my ability to perform (my very best one
inevitably wrecks my voice every time I do it).  Ten years ago I was lucky to
get one such event in eighteen months.  They're rarer now.

My fourth gift from the Gods was other pagans.

In late 1977 a woman I was involved with, who'd seen some of my odd gifts and
shared something of my developing understanding of them, invited me to go up
to New York City with her to meet some friends.  She was singularly mysterious
about them, and kept smiling as though she knew something I didn't.

She did.  They were a Wiccan coven called Blue Star.  And they knew what I
was.  They'd seen it before.  And they had a whole technology for raising
the God and Goddess and using their power that I hadn't known existed.

Now, understand, I hadn't thought of myself as a witch up to then.  To
the extent I labeled myself at all, I thought of myself as a shaman or
mystic.  I didn't use ritual.  I didn't work with other people.  I knew
very little about Wicca.

They showed me that the imagery and energy I'd been using was part of a larger
picture; that it fit the mythology of Wicca and inspired the Wiccan ritual
forms.  They showed me that I was a natural witch, albeit of a kind uncommon
in this century.  Most Wiccan clergy today, and most of its influential
mystics, are female.  For a man to spontaneously channel the God before
training is unusual, though not unheard-of.

Subsequently, I made numerous friends in the neo-pagan movement.  (The Wiccans
are the largest single group in that movement.)  I went to many festivals, and
founded and ran a coven of my own for six years.

Neopagans are special people.  We attract the best and the worst; the geniuses
and the borderline nutcases.  No two are alike and I've had bitter disputes
with a few.  One thing they never are is boring.  Because each and every one
got to be a pagan by reaching beyond what our mainstream culture and consensus
social "reality" thinks it knows.  They're kin to extropians that way.

My fifth gift from the Gods was wisdom.

Wisdom includes being able to see individual people with all their flaws, and
humanity with all its flaws, and love them and it anyway.  And not with any
distant, superior, patronizing abstract "love" but with the personal love that
says "YES!  Your joy is my joy.  My struggle is your struggle!  We are *all*
made of bone and breath and flesh and blood and godhood all tangled
together.  AND IT IS GOOD!"

Wisdom includes the understanding that intellect by itself is never enough.
We were hominids and primates and mammals and animals and vertebrates and
eukaryotes before we were "rational" beings.  We have needs that can't be
filled by abstractions; we have important abilities that vest in the 99% of
our minds that isn't the evolutionarily-recent subsystem called
"consciousness".  And if we don't learn to use these aspects of self, they
will assuredly use *us*.  

Wisdom includes the gut-level comprehension that we create the Gods no less
and no more than they create us.  The Gods, viewed one way, are symbolic
projections of our neurological and cultural wiring.  Viewed another way, they
are forces (or a set of options) which underpin and create both individual
personality and most of what we call art.  Theism and atheism both miss the
point!

The Gods taught me these things while I was being some of them.  Usually I've
been the Horned Lord (Pan/Cernunnos/Freyr/Krishna).  Occasionally I've been
the Trickster (Coyote/Mercury/Loki/Eris) or the Sage (Thoth/Merlin).  Just
once, by accident in a martial-arts class, I have been the Warrior
(Tyr/Indra/Cuchulain).

Of course, the question Michael was really asking wasn't "What's in it for
you?" but "What might be in it for me?" :-)

We neopagans regard that as a very proper question.  A religion that's no use
is no use.  Or, to put it another way, putting energy into a god-form is an
investment.  If you don't get any bennies out of it, must be a pretty
piss-poor god.

So what can Wicca do for you?

I can't promise that it will give you musical ability.  You may not have the
neural machinery for that.

Nor can I promise that it'll juice up your sex life, though that's more
likely than the above.

It is *quite* likely that it will bring out whatever poetic ability you may
have.  (This effect is so widely known in the neopagan movement that there are
jokes about it.)

You will meet a lot of fascinating people.  And a few utter flakes. :-)

One effect I haven't mentioned in the personal history above is that you might
learn how to do healing with your hands.  Happens I learned this outside the
neopagan movement (from some New Agers, in fact) but it's a widespread skill
within Wicca.  (Don't laugh; they teach it in nursing schools now, too.)

If nothing else --- well, dancing naked around a bonfire is pretty neat,
actually.  At midnight.  With forty or fifty hand-drummers making like thunder
in the heart of Africa.  And chanting.  "Peak experience" doesn't even *begin*
to cover it.

What Robert Bly and the "men's movement" crowd are doing is a knockoff of
neopagan ecstaticism for middle-aged white males afraid of religious
overtones.  Ditto for a lot of what's called "feminist spirituality", though
that tends to sidle closer to real neopaganism and in places to overlap it.
Even these denatured versions are useful --- but nowhere *near* as powerful as
the real thing.

If you think you're too "rational" or "mature" or "civilized" for such an
an experience, all I can say is you ought to try it --- and I truly pity you
if you're right.

Because the fact is that my life as a neopagan has made me a *better* man of
reason than I was.  I never abandoned my scientific world-view; I never had
to.  So what if the Old Gods are just "independent complexes" or "archetypes"
or whatever else you want to call an old, strong meme with hooks into our
neurology and evolutionary past?  So what if they're only mobilizing
capabilities that were latent in me and others all along?  What matters is
that *they can be invoked*.  And I know how.

Dancing with the Gods has made me a better, wiser, stronger, sexier, more
loving, and clearer-eyed person than I was before.  Or, I believe, would have
been otherwise.  Wicca married my intellect to the earthy reality of human
experience; I'm free to be both hacker and poet, technician and musician,
careful philosopher and leaping ecstatic mystic.  A whole man --- and
sometimes, when it matters, something more.
-- 

