"James A. Donald" says:

> > You are unfamiliar with everything you comment on, Mr. Donald. 
> > 
> > I note, for the benefit of others who might not have quite so solid a
> > set of bogosity detectors, that Mr. Donald's postings should be viewed
> > with a great deal of skepticism.
> 
> I have raised specific facts:  That there exist molecules
> analogous to the mechanisms proposed by Drexler, for
> example HOPG, and fullerene spheres sitting on HOPG planes,
> and they do not move around freely from thermal motion, as
> would follow from Drexler's assumptions, they freeze up, as
> Drexlers unlubricated nanomachines would freeze up.

Pardon, but this is all very bogus. What you are essentially claiming
is "the world contains solids". Yes, you can form solids. What of it?
Was this a secret? A five year old could tell you that.  Does Drexler
ever claim that solids do not exist in the world?  No.

James A. Donald keeps asserting that nanomachines can't possibly work.
We hear about how "friction destroys micromachines", never mind that
silicon micromachines such as working motors and the like have been
built and work fine, and that Drexler is talking about nanomachines,
not micromachines. (Mr. Donald will now claim "no, they don't work",
but never mind that, he can't give us a single reference, and I can
give you all the references you like. Try me.)

James A. Donald keeps on asserting that Drexler's machines will have
problems from "static friction", a phenomenon that isn't usually
thought of in connection with individual molecules. He keeps claiming
Drexler's mathematics showing that the problem isn't real is "flawed",
but he can't tell us what the flaws are beyond handwaving. He provides
no equations, states no references, in short, just asserts blindly. A
reading of Drexlers books will show that Drexler has done his homework
-- but of course Mr. Donald shows no sign of having read the books.

The fact remains that there is proof all around us, in the form of our
own bodies, that nanomachines work. Mr. Donald claims "Drexlers
machines are bad because they are too rigid" or "Drexlers nanomachines
can't work because they experience too much friction" and all that
sort of thing, but he gives us no proof, and there seems to be proof
in the form of living things all around us that he's just plain wrong.
Even were a few of Drexlers engineering ideas bad there are a wealth
of other possibilities that likely will work -- we are literally
living proof of this.

