Notes on the seminar:  The discussion here is at least as good as
any I've seen in the many "real" seminars I've attended, but
the number of active participants is fewer.  Though Tim has been
doing an admirable job of providing reading material on-line, we
do suffer from the lack of seminar-style "paper presentation" talks
that participants give.  Then again, we're not paying hundreds of
dollars to participate like we would do in a "real" seminar.  Still,
I am taking it quite seriously and am getting lots out of it.  It
would help if we had more direction.  In particular, if our most
important primary goal is to make sure that we have a good grasp
of the technical issues involved in the basic AIT formalisms, we
should focus on that kind of thing.  I can make up homework problems!
Would anybody do them?

One thing that's been done with some effectiveness in seminars
I've taken in the past is to have every participant write up
a one-page analysis of "assigned" papers.  Except that I doubt
anybody would actually do it, it could work well here.

Lacking such a focus, I happily continue in the normal "reach
exceeds grasp" mode!

Thanks, Hal, for your quite interesting series of recent posts on
AIT.  I hope that a few other people are reading and thinking about
the material.  I'm purposefully holding back my posting rate on
this thread to avoid burnout.

I agree with the interest in Logical Depth as an (at least metaphorical)
objective measure of complexity.  Despite that, I'd like to raise an
objection that I hope we can answer in a satisfactory way.  We have
to bridge the gap between strings computed by turing machines and
physical objects arranged by physical processes.  I am uncomfortable
with the usual way of addressing this -- a physical church's thesis
saying that the physics can be simulated by a turing machine, and
since all turing machines are "equivalent" we're ok.  For Bennett's
purpose -- the broader (quite extropian!) task of studying whether
physical complexity can increase without bound given infinite
space and time -- mundane questions of the sort we're discussing
aren't too important.

My question, following the thread that Robin started, is that while
it's true that there's this "theoretically trivial" penalty for
switching from one machine definition to another, I'm concerned
about cases where the value is so large as to swamp the complexity
of the objects under study.  In particular, if we want to use a
physical (perhaps evolutionary) analogue of Logical Depth to define
physical complexity, we'd want different Darwin Machines to be
similarly trivial translations.  However, I'm concerned that the
translation may be much more time-consuming than the generating
program, making comparisons of logical depth for things of low
complexity (like us) difficult, and once again requiring the
introduction of context-dependent features to make comparisons
meaningful (the approach I still favor anyway).

> So, it appears to me that evolutionary processes may be characterized by
> the production of logical depth.

The above caveat aside, this seems reasonable to me.  Evolution
searches a "space" of possible objects.  The chances of a starting
state being the endpoint (if there is one) of that search is
quite small and evolution uses lots of work to do that search.
Hal's earlier points about the necessity of irreversibility for
an increase of logical depth also seem right on target -- in those
cases where higher fitness is rarer (that is, higher fitness values
have fewer object specifications that qualify), evolution reduces
the phase space of objects as fitness increases -- an irreversible,
dissipative process.  This relationship between dissipative processes
and self-organization is, I believe, well established (see Prigogine).

In fact, it may offer a general answer to the question that Hal wants
answered:

> This would suggest that there are some kinds of physical processes which
> increase logical depth, and some which don't.  What distinguishes them?

Proposal:

  Whether the dominant system-evolution forces drive toward
  smaller or larger configuration spaces.
  
In the case of celestial mechanics, the second law of thermodynamics
guides system evolution; we're looking for maximum entropy.  This
(generally locally and of course always globally) leads to larger
phase space volumes.  Systems whose configuration-evolution pressures
are toward smaller regions of possible configurations can make use of
locally dissipative mechanisms to increase logical depth.  Natural
evolution is one example.  Others include the design of airplanes and
other devices.

derek


